an American tourist who was held captive  in war-ravaged Syria for two months is  finally free  Sam Goodwin of st. Louis was released  following secret negotiations involving  a member of the Lebanese military CBS  News State Department reporter Christina  Ruffini has the latest on the man who  had been on a journey across the globe  his mission was simple Sam Goodwin would  not return home until he'd visited every  country in the world and there were  apparently no exceptions  not even Syria where the bloody  seemingly intractable Civil War has  killed an estimated half a million  people in the last eight years  unable to get an official visa the 30  year old entered the country through a  Kurdish controlled checkpoint in the  northeast reportedly wandered the  streets without a guide and was  eventually picked up by Syrian  government forces he was convicted of  illegal entry and spent more than two  months in custody it puts a lot of  things into perspective that's probably  the biggest thing that that I learned  and it really makes you appreciate  everything that you have here Goodwin  who grew up in st. Louis has been an  avid traveler since college in 2012 he  told his campus TV station a trip to  Africa had changed his perspective on  life coming off an experience like that  you really realize that you're what you  consider problems are really maybe not  as severe as you may think they are good  ones detention inside Syria was not  widely known his surprise release  yesterday was secured with the help of a  Lebanese general who negotiated with the  Syrian government on behalf of his  family Goodwin was reunited with his  parents overseas we are forever indebted  to Lebanese general Abbas Ibrahim and to  all others who helped secure the release  of our son they said in a statement the  62 days Goodwin spent in custody may  seem like a long time but it's actually  relatively short for these kinds of  detentions which can drag on for years  everyone we spoke to yesterday both  inside  outside the government said they were  thrilled by the outcome Dana sources  tell CBS News no ransom was paid for his  release Christina thank you
 

